        <div id="caffeine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which Premier Protein Flavors Have Caffeine?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three ready-to-drink flavors have caffeine: Café Latte (sold as "+Energy"), Coffeehouse Mocha and Coffeehouse Caramel Macchiato, plus the Ultimate 42g Café Latte. Premier says each has as much caffeine as a cup of coffee, and each label lists coffee extract and caffeine. Premier does not publish a milligram figure for the shakes on its product pages.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Café Latte protein powder is the one product with a stated number: Premier's product page gives 95 mg of caffeine per two-scoop serving. For context, the US Food and Drug Administration says up to about 400 mg a day is not generally linked to negative effects in healthy adults, so one caffeinated shake plus a coffee is well within that, while three caffeinated shakes plus coffee may not be.
          </p>

          <p>
            Every other Premier flavor is caffeine-free, including Chocolate and the chocolate-based Indulgence flavors, which contain cocoa but not added caffeine. The Almondmilk Coffee flavor is a separate line; check its label before assuming it is caffeine-free or caffeinated. The sparkling protein sodas have no caffeine, per Premier.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Caffeinated flavor before noon, classic flavor after</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Keep Café Latte, Mocha and Caramel Macchiato for mornings or before a workout, and drink Caramel, Chocolate or Vanilla later in the day. A late caffeinated shake can cost you sleep, which matters more for recovery than the protein. If you are pregnant, caffeine-sensitive or on medication that interacts with stimulants, ask your doctor before using them.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Between the two main caffeinated options, Café Latte is the cheaper one, at about $2.12 a shake against about $2.67 for Caramel Macchiato at the Amazon prices we saw. Tasting Table rated Caramel Macchiato the best Premier flavor overall, and Sporked rated Café Latte the best of the eight it tried, so either is a reasonable first buy. Choose by whether you take your coffee plain or sweet.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you would rather stir protein into your own coffee, a powder made for heat is a better fit than a bottled shake. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-coffee/">best protein powder for coffee</a> guide ranks the powders that mix into hot coffee without clumping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="indulgence" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Is the Premier Protein Indulgence Line Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Indulgence line is worth it if the classic shakes taste thin to you, but it costs more and adds sugar. Per Premier's labels at the time of writing, Indulgence shakes have 170 calories, 3 g of sugar, 2 g of added sugar and 7 g of carbohydrate per 11.5 oz shake, against 160 calories and 0 g of added sugar for the Classic line. Protein stays at 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier describes Indulgence as a richer, creamier texture with flavors inspired by chocolate confections, and the ingredient lists back that up: a small amount of sugar appears on the label, which the Classic shakes do not have. Published rankings rate the line unevenly. Tasting Table placed Chocolate Covered Strawberry second and Salted Caramel Truffle third of 16, but put White Chocolate Raspberry 15th, comparing its raspberry note to cough syrup, and Decadent Dark Chocolate 12th. ThirstyBear ranked Decadent Dark Chocolate first of 19.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salted Caramel Truffle is our pick from the line because it does well in both rankings that cover it in depth and has the clearest reason to exist, a salty-sweet balance the Classic Caramel does not offer. At about $2.89 a shake at the Amazon price we saw, it is the most expensive of our seven picks, so it works best as a dessert swap rather than a daily breakfast.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a Salted Caramel Truffle Indulgence powder. Per Premier's label it gives 30 g of protein, 160 calories, 2 g of sugar and 1 g of added sugar in two scoops, and it sold for $38.48 for 17 servings on Amazon when we checked, about $1.89 per 25 g of protein. Its Amazon title calls it 100% whey protein isolate, but Premier's own ingredient list shows whey isolate and whey concentrate, so trust the brand label. Our <a href="/best-salted-caramel-protein-powder/">best salted caramel protein powder</a> guide compares it with other caramel powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are counting added sugar strictly, for example on a diabetes plan, the Classic flavors are the better daily choice. Two grams of added sugar is small, but the Classic shakes give the same protein with none.
          </p>
        </div>

